 To obtain the constant $c,$ take the dot product of $\mathbf{v}$ with the normal vector to the plane, which is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ c \end{pmatrix}.$  This gives us
\[\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ c \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\ 3t \\ 1 - t \end{pmatrix} = 0.\]This simplifies to $c = -5.$

When $t = 0,$ $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ a \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},$ and $a$ is the plane $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ c \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ a \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ crossed by the line.  Thus, $a + c = d,$ or $a - 5 = d.$

When $t = 1,$ $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix},$ and this vector lies in the plane.  Hence, $2 + 3 + 0 = 5 = d,$ so $(c,d) = \boxed{(-5,5)}.$

